Here is a sample code for the microservice that implements the four core functionalities: creating, updating, deleting, and retrieving resources with a focus on operational resilience and efficient resource utilization:
```scss
package com.example.resourceservice;

import java.util.UUID;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import com.example.api.ExternalAPI;
import com.example.model.Resource;
import com.example.exception.ResourceNotFoundException;
import com.example.exception.ExternalAPIException;

@Service
public class ResourceService {

    @Autowired
    private ExternalAPI api;

    public void createResource(Resource resource) throws ExternalAPIException, ResourceAlreadyExistsException {
        // Generate a unique UUID for the resource
        resource.setId(UUID.randomUUID().toString());

        // Validate and transform the data to match the external API requirements
        validateAndTransformData(resource);

        // Create the resource on the external API
        api.createResource(resource);
    }

    public void updateResource(String id, Resource updatedResource) throws ExternalAPIException, ResourceNotFoundException {
        // Validate and transform the data to match the external API requirements
        validateAndTransformData(updatedResource);

        // Retrieve the resource from the external API
        Resource existingResource = api.getResource(id);

        // Check for conflicting updates
        if (!existingResource.equals(updatedResource)) {
            // Handle conflict resolution
            handleConflictResolution(id, updatedResource, existingResource);
        } else {
            // Update the resource on the external API
            api.updateResource(id, updatedResource);
        }
    }

    public void deleteResource(String id) throws ExternalAPIException, ResourceNotFoundException {
        // Retrieve the resource from the external API
        Resource existingResource = api.getResource(id);

        // Check for dependencies and handle cascading deletion if necessary
        checkForDependencies(existingResource);

        // Soft delete the resource on the external API
        api.softDeleteResource(id);
    }

    public Resource retrieveResource(String id) throws ExternalAPIException, ResourceNotFoundException {
        // Retrieve the resource from the external API
        Resource resource = api.getResource(id);

        // Compute and securely store the MD5 hash of the resource identifier for efficient lookups
        computeAndSecurelyStoreMD5Hash(resource);

        // Leverage caching strategies to enhance performance, balancing cache size and hit rate against memory usage
        if (cache.getIfPresent(id) != null) {
            return cache.getIfPresent(id);
        } else {
            // Retrieve the resource from the external API if not found in the cache
            Resource retrievedResource = api.getResource(id);

            // Cache the retrieved resource for future use
            cache.put(id, retrievedResource);

            return retrievedResource;
        }
    }

    private void validateAndTransformData(Resource resource) {
        // Validate the data to match the external API requirements
        if (resource.getId() == null || !UUID.fromString(resource.getId()).toString().equals(resource.getId())) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid resource ID");
        }

        // Transform the data to match the external API requirements
        resource.setName(resource.getName().toLowerCase());
    }

    private void handleConflictResolution(String id, Resource updatedResource, Resource existingResource) {
        // Handle conflict resolution by merging the updated and existing resources
        Resource mergedResource = mergeResources(updatedResource, existingResource);

        // Update the resource on the external API with the merged resource
        api.updateResource(id, mergedResource);
    }

    private void checkForDependencies(Resource resource) {
        // Check for dependencies on other resources and handle cascading deletion if necessary
        if (resource.getDependency1() != null) {
            deleteResource(resource.getDependency1());
        }

        if (resource.getDependency2() != null) {
            deleteResource(resource.getDependency2());
        }
    }

    private Resource mergeResources(Resource updatedResource, Resource existingResource) {
        // Merge the updated and existing resources
        Resource mergedResource = new Resource();

        // Set the merged resource ID to the existing resource ID
        mergedResource.setId(existingResource.getId());

        // Set the merged resource name to the updated resource name if it is not null, otherwise set it to the existing resource name
        if (updatedResource.getName() != null) {
            mergedResource.setName(updatedResource.getName());
        } else {
            mergedResource.setName(existingResource.getName());
        }

        // Set the merged resource description to the updated resource description if it is not null, otherwise set it to the existing resource description
        if (updatedResource.getDescription() != null) {
            mergedResource.setDescription(updatedResource.getDescription());
        } else {
            mergedResource.setDescription(existingResource.getDescription());
        }

        return mergedResource;
    }
}
```
This code uses Spring Framework to implement the service, with dependencies on external APIs and a cache layer for efficient resource retrieval. The `validateAndTransformData` method ensures that the data is validated and transformed to match the external API requirements, while the `handleConflictResolution` method handles conflicts by merging the updated and existing resources. The `checkForDependencies` method checks for dependencies on other resources and handles cascading deletion if necessary. The `mergeResources` method merges the updated and existing resources.

The service is designed to be highly available and scalable, with a focus on operational resilience and efficient resource utilization. It uses a cache layer to enhance performance, balancing cache size and hit rate against memory usage. The service also uses soft deletion to handle cascading deletion of dependent resources, which helps ensure data consistency and avoids orphaned resources.']

