### C++ Implementation

Here is an example of how the `Encryption` interface could be implemented in C++:

```cpp
class Encryption {
public:
    virtual void encrypt(const std::string& plaintext, std::string* ciphertext) const = 0;
    virtual void decrypt(const std::string& ciphertext, std::string* plaintext) const = 0;
};
```

This interface provides two pure virtual functions: `encrypt` and `decrypt`. These functions take the plaintext and ciphertext as input, and return the encrypted or decrypted text in a `std::string` object. The `Encryption` class is then used to create different encryption algorithms, such as AES-128, AES-192, and AES-256.

Here is an example of how one could implement the AES-128 algorithm using the `Encryption` interface:

```cpp
class Aes128 : public Encryption {
public:
    void encrypt(const std::string& plaintext, std::string* ciphertext) const override;
    void decrypt(const std::string& ciphertext, std::string* plaintext) const override;
};
```

This class provides two functions that implement the encryption and decryption of AES-128. These functions take the plaintext and ciphertext as input, and return the encrypted or decrypted text in a `std::string` object.

Here is an example of how one could use these classes to encrypt and decrypt data:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string plaintext = "This is a secret message.";
    Encryption* encryption = new Aes128();
    std::string ciphertext;
    encryption->encrypt(plaintext, &ciphertext);
    std::cout << "Encrypted text: " << ciphertext << std::endl;

    std::string decrypted_text;
    encryption->decrypt(ciphertext, &decrypted_text);
    std::cout << "Decrypted text: " << decrypted_text << std::endl;
}
```

This program creates an instance of the `Aes128` class and uses it to encrypt and decrypt a message. The encrypted text is printed to the console, along with the decrypted text.']

