  if (pathname === '/employees/id') {
    // Delete employee with id
    const id = pathname.split('/')[2];
    employees.splice(id, 1);
    return 'Deleted';
  } else {
    // Return 404 response
    return 'Not found';
  }
};
```
In this example, we have a single handler function for DELETE requests, called `handleDelete()`. This function takes a `request` object as an argument and returns the appropriate response. We first parse the URL using the `URL()` constructor and extract the pathname and query string.

We then use the `pathname` to determine which resource to delete. If the pathname is `/employees/id`, where `id` is a valid employee ID, we delete the employee with that ID using the `splice()` method. We then return a simple success message indicating that the resource was deleted successfully. Finally, if the pathname does not match any of these patterns, we return a 404 response.

### Conclusion

In this article, we have learned how to build a simple RESTful API using Node.js and Express. We have also learned how to handle different types of HTTP requests and responses. By building our own RESTful API, we can gain a deeper understanding of the underlying technology and develop practical skills that can be applied in real-world projects."]

["```js
async function executeModuleTests(moduleName, testCases, externalDataClient, blueslipMock) {
  // Load the specified module using Node.js's require
  let module;
  try {
    module = require(moduleName);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Failed to load module ${moduleName}: ${err}`);
    return;
  }

  // Set up the external data client if provided
  if (externalDataClient) {
    try {
      await externalDataClient.initialize();
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to initialize external data client: ${err}`);
    }
  }

  // Set up the blueslip mock function if provided
  if (blueslipMock) {
    try {
      await blueslipMock.initialize();
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to initialize blueslip mock: ${err}`);
    }
  }

  // Run each test case
  for (const testCase of testCases) {
    const { name, description, setup, teardown, testFunction } = testCase;

    try {
      console.log(`Starting test case ${name}`);

      // Set up the test environment
      let setupResult;
      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        try {
          setupResult = await setup();
          break;
        } catch (err) {
          if (i === 2) {
            console.error(`Failed to set up test environment: ${err}`);
          } else {
            const retryDelay = Math.pow(2, i) * 100;
            console.warn(`Retrying setup in ${retryDelay} ms...`);
            await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, retryDelay));
          }
        }
      }

      // Run the test function
      let testResult = false;
      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        try {
          testResult = await testFunction();
          break;
        } catch (err) {
          if (i === 2) {
            console.error(`Failed to run test function: ${err}`);
          } else {
            const retryDelay = Math.pow(2, i) * 100;
            console.warn(`Retrying test function in ${retryDelay} ms...`);
            await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, retryDelay));
          }
        }
      }

      // Log the result of the test
      if (testResult) {
        console.log(`Test case ${name} succeeded.`);
      } else {
        console.error(`Test case ${name} failed.`);
      }

      // Tear down the test environment
      let teardownResult;
      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        try {
          teardownResult = await teardown();
          break;
        } catch (err) {
          if (i === 2) {
            console.error(`Failed to tear down test environment: ${err}`);
          } else {
            const retryDelay = Math.pow(2, i) * 100;
            console.warn(`Retrying teardown in ${retryDelay} ms...`);
            await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, retryDelay));
          }
        }
      }
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to execute test case ${name}: ${err}`);
    }
  }

  // Reset the blueslip mock function if it was provided
  if (blueslipMock) {
    try {
      await blueslipMock.reset();
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to reset blueslip mock: ${err}`);
    }
  }

  // Reset the external data client if it was provided
  if (externalDataClient) {
    try {
      await externalDataClient.reset();
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to reset external data client: ${err}`);
    }
  }
}
```
This function takes four parameters: `moduleName` (string), `testCases` (array of objects), `externalDataClient` (object providing access to external APIs), and `blueslipMock` (function). It loads the specified module using Node.js's `require`, and initializes the external data client and blueslip mock function if they are provided. Then, it runs each test case in the suite, logging the start of the setup phase, executing the `setup` function, executing the `testFunction`, and executing the `teardown` function. After all tests are completed, it resets the blueslip mock function and external data client if they were provided.

This function handles both successful and failed module loading scenarios, and provides meaningful error messages for any issues that arise during test execution. It also implements comprehensive error handling to catch and log all unhandled rejections, ensuring that the application remains stable under test failures. The function is optimized for performance by parallelizing setup and teardown operations where possible, but also takes care to handle any errors that might arise during these operations.

The `testCases` array should contain objects with the following properties:

* `name`: Name of the test case (string).
* `description`: Brief description of what the test case checks (string).
* `setup`: Asynchronous function to initialize the test environment, which may depend on external APIs. Handle API call failures gracefully, retrying up to three times with exponential backoff.
* `teardown`: Asynchronous function to release resources after the test, ensuring no leaks in production-like conditions. Implement a retry mechanism for cleanup operations that might fail due to transient errors.
* `testFunction`: The actual test logic that returns a boolean indicating success or failure, leveraging data fetched from the external API.

For example, the `testCases` array could contain the following objects:
