This code uses `sizeof` to get the size of the array in bytes and divides it by the size of an integer to get the number of elements in the array. It then checks if the number of states is greater than the maximum number of elements in the array, and returns if it is. If not, it allocates memory for the new states using `malloc`, copies the old states into the new array, reallocates the array to accommodate more states using `realloc`, and sets the new states in the FSA. Finally, it frees the old memory after reallocating it.

This function is thread-safe because it uses a critical section to manage the allocation and deallocation of memory for the FSA's state array. This ensures that only one thread can access the array at a time, preventing race conditions and data corruption.']

