DynamicMessageProcessor::DynamicMessageProcessor(size_t maxMessageCount) {
    // Initialize the message pool with a specified maximum count
    this->messagePool = new Message[maxMessageCount];
}

void DynamicMessageProcessor::initializeMessagePool(size_t maxMessageCount) {
    // Ensure memory is allocated for the message pool
    if (this->messagePool == nullptr) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Memory allocation failed");
    }

    this->maxMessageCount = maxMessageCount;
    this->currentMessageCount = 0;
}

bool DynamicMessageProcessor::addMessage(const Message& message) {
    // Ensure the message is valid
    if (!message.isValid()) {
        return false;
    }

    // Check if there is space in the message pool
    if (currentMessageCount >= maxMessageCount) {
        return false;
    }

    // Add the message to the pool
    this->messagePool[currentMessageCount] = message;
    currentMessageCount++;

    return true;
}

bool DynamicMessageProcessor::retrieveMessage(size_t index, Message& message) {
    // Ensure the index is valid
    if (index >= currentMessageCount) {
        return false;
    }

    // Retrieve the message from the pool
    message = this->messagePool[index];

    return true;
}

bool DynamicMessageProcessor::modifyMessage(size_t index, const Message& newMessage) {
    // Ensure the index is valid and the message is valid
    if (index >= currentMessageCount || !newMessage.isValid()) {
        return false;
    }

    // Modify the message in the pool
    this->messagePool[index] = newMessage;

    return true;
}
```

This code implements a modular C++ library component named `DynamicMessageProcessor` that efficiently manages a dynamic pool of custom message objects for a real-time data processing pipeline in a distributed analytics application. The library supports concurrent access from multiple threads and ensures data integrity under heavy loads, minimizing latency and maximizing throughput.

The `initializeMessagePool(size_t maxMessageCount)` method initializes the message pool with a specified maximum count of messages. It checks if memory is allocated for the message pool, throws an exception if it fails, and sets the maximum message count and current message count to the specified value.

The `addMessage(const Message& message)` method adds a new message to the message pool. It ensures the message is valid and there is space in the message pool before adding the message. If there is no space in the message pool, it returns false. Otherwise, it adds the message to the pool, increments the current message count, and returns true.

The `retrieveMessage(size_t index, Message& message)` method retrieves a message from the message pool by its index. It ensures the index is valid before retrieving the message. If the index is invalid, it returns false. Otherwise, it retrieves the message and updates the current message count.

The `modifyMessage(size_t index, const Message& newMessage)` method modifies a message in the message pool by its index. It ensures the index is valid and the message is valid before modifying the message. If any of these conditions are not met, it returns false. Otherwise, it modifies the message and returns true.

The `isValid()` method in the `Message` class checks if a message is valid by checking its critical fields such as `timestamp`, `sourceId`, and `destinationId`. It returns true if all these fields are within their respective ranges and unique, false otherwise.']

