using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Text;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.Linq;
using DynamicXmlParser.Caching;
using DynamicXmlParser.ErrorHandling;
using DynamicXmlParser.Logging;
using DynamicXmlParser.Parsing;
using DynamicXmlParser.Retry;
using DynamicXmlParser.Utils;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

namespace DynamicXmlParser
{
    public class Parser
    {
        private readonly ICache _cache;
        private readonly IErrorHandler _errorHandler;
        private readonly ILogger _logger;
        private readonly IParsingStrategy _parsingStrategy;
        private readonly IRetryPolicy _retryPolicy;
        private readonly IDistributedTracer _distributedTracer;

        public Parser(ICache cache, IErrorHandler errorHandler, ILogger logger, IParsingStrategy parsingStrategy, IRetryPolicy retryPolicy, IDistributedTracer distributedTracer)
        {
            _cache = cache;
            _errorHandler = errorHandler;
            _logger = logger;
            _parsingStrategy = parsingStrategy;
            _retryPolicy = retryPolicy;
            _distributedTracer = distributedTracer;
        }

        public T Parse<T>(string xml, string endpoint = null) where T : class
        {
            try
            {
                var key = GetCacheKey(xml, endpoint);
                if (_cache.Contains(key))
                {
                    return (T)_cache[key];
                }
                else
                {
                    _logger.LogInformation($"Parsing XML string from {endpoint ?? "local file"}");
                    var parser = new XmlParser();
                    var parsedXml = parser.Parse(xml);
                    var result = _parsingStrategy.GetObject<T>(parsedXml, endpoint);
                    _cache.Add(key, result);
                    return result;
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                _errorHandler.HandleError(ex, "Error parsing XML string");
                throw;
            }
        }

        public T Parse<T>(Stream xmlStream, string endpoint = null) where T : class
        {
            try
            {
                var key = GetCacheKey(xmlStream, endpoint);
                if (_cache.Contains(key))
                {
                    return (T)_cache[key];
                }
                else
                {
                    _logger.LogInformation($"Parsing XML stream from {endpoint ?? "local file"}");
                    var parser = new XmlParser();
                    var parsedXml = parser.Parse(xmlStream);
                    var result = _parsingStrategy.GetObject<T>(parsedXml, endpoint);
                    _cache.Add(key, result);
                    return result;
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                _errorHandler.HandleError(ex, "Error parsing XML stream");
                throw;
            }
        }

        private string GetCacheKey(string xmlString, string endpoint = null) => $"{endpoint ?? "local_file"}_{XmlUtils.GetHashCode(xmlString)}";

        private string GetCacheKey(Stream xmlStream, string endpoint = null) => $"{endpoint ?? "local_file"}_{XmlUtils.GetHashCode(xmlStream)}";
    }
}
```

### Caching Strategy

The caching strategy used in the `DynamicXmlParser` library is based on an LRU eviction policy, which combines in-memory and disk-based storage to manage cache sizes effectively. The cache is designed to support scheduled flushes based on predefined conditions such as schema changes or time intervals, ensuring data consistency and reducing latency.

The caching strategy is implemented using the `ICache` interface, which provides a set of methods for adding, removing, and retrieving cached items. The cache is thread-safe, allowing concurrent access to the parser with high concurrency and performance, particularly when processing large volumes of small XML documents. Advanced locking strategies are used to minimize contention and maximize throughput.

### Error Handling

The `DynamicXmlParser` library uses robust error handling and logging mechanisms to ensure reliable data flow and error propagation. The error handling mechanism is designed to handle transient network errors and configurable backoff strategies, along with custom health checks and monitoring metrics for external dependencies. The library also integrates with a centralized logging service for monitoring and analysis.

Error handling is implemented using the `IErrorHandler` interface, which provides a set of methods for handling errors in a generic way. The error handler can be configured to handle different types of errors, such as network errors or parsing errors, and can also retry failed requests based on configurable backoff strategies.

### Integration with External Systems

The `DynamicXmlParser` library supports seamless integration with external systems, including REST APIs, databases, and third-party services. The library uses retry logic and circuit breaker patterns to handle service outages or rate limits, ensuring reliable data flow and error propagation. Custom health checks and monitoring metrics are also integrated to ensure the overall system's reliability and availability.

Integration with external systems is implemented using the `IExternalSystem` interface, which provides a set of methods for interacting with external systems in a generic way. The library can be configured to use different types of external systems, such as REST APIs or message queues, and can also handle different types of errors and responses.

### Design Patterns

The `DynamicXmlParser` library uses design patterns such as Factory and Strategy to enhance flexibility and reusability. The factory pattern is used to create instances of the parsing strategy, allowing for easy configuration and extension of the parser's behavior. The strategy pattern is used to define different parsing strategies, such as parsing XML strings or streams, allowing for multiple implementation options.

### Performance Optimizations

The `DynamicXmlParser` library has been optimized for performance through various techniques, including lazy loading and streaming techniques. Lazy loading involves loading data from external systems only when it is needed, reducing the amount of data that needs to be processed and improving overall performance. Streaming techniques are used to process large amounts of data in a streaming fashion, allowing for faster processing times.

The library also uses caching mechanisms to improve performance by reducing the number of requests made to external systems. Caching is implemented using the `ICache` interface, which provides a set of methods for adding, removing, and retrieving cached items. The cache is designed to support scheduled flushes based on predefined conditions, ensuring data consistency and reducing latency.

### Testing

The `DynamicXmlParser` library has been thoroughly tested through various testing mechanisms, including unit tests, integration tests, and performance tests. Unit tests are used to test the functionality of individual components of the parser, while integration tests are used to test the interaction between different components of the parser. Performance tests are used to measure the performance of the parser under different conditions, such as high load or large data sets.

The library is also designed to be modular and extensible, allowing for easy addition of new features and functionality through the use of plugins and other mechanisms. This allows for a more flexible and adaptable approach to parsing XML data, making it easier to maintain and extend over time.']

