Here is an example of how you could implement this modular Node.js service using a distributed task queue like RabbitMQ or Kafka:
```
// Define the MIME types to filter on
const mimeTypes = ['image/jpeg', 'image/png'];

// Set up the RabbitMQ connection and channel
const amqp = require('amqplib');
const connection = await amqp.connect('amqp://localhost');
const channel = await connection.createChannel();

// Define the queue to use for distributing file processing tasks
const queueName = 'file_processing';
await channel.assertQueue(queueName, {
  durable: true,
});

// Set up the file system watcher to monitor the specified directory
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const watcher = fs.watch(path.resolve(__dirname, 'files'), {
  persistent: false,
}, (eventType, filename) => {
  if (eventType === 'change') {
    const filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'files', filename);
    // Check if the file is a valid MIME type and add it to the queue for processing
    const mimeType = fs.statSync(filePath).mimeType;
    if (mimeTypes.includes(mimeType)) {
      channel.sendToQueue(queueName, Buffer.from(filename));
    }
  }
});

// Define a function to process the files in the queue
const processFile = async (message) => {
  const filename = message.content.toString();
  try {
    // Process the file
    console.log(`Processing file ${filename}`);
    // ...
    // Add any additional processing logic here
    // ...
    channel.ack(message);
  } catch (error) {
    channel.nack(message, false, true);
  }
};

// Start consuming messages from the queue
channel.consume(queueName, processFile, { noAck: false });
```
In this example, we set up a RabbitMQ connection and channel to use for distributing file processing tasks. We define a queue with the name `file_processing` and set it to be durable so that messages are not lost if the service crashes or restarts.

We then set up a file system watcher to monitor the specified directory for changes, using the `fs.watch` function from the Node.js standard library. Whenever a change is detected (e.g., a new file is created), we check if the file is a valid MIME type and add it to the queue for processing if it is.

We define a function called `processFile` that takes a message from the queue as an argument and processes the corresponding file. We log any errors that occur during processing and use the RabbitMQ acknowledgement mechanism (`channel.ack`) to confirm that the message has been processed successfully. If any errors are encountered, we use the RabbitMQ negative acknowledgement mechanism (`channel.nack`) to requeue the message for later processing.

Finally, we start consuming messages from the `file_processing` queue using the `channel.consume` function and pass in our `processFile` function as a callback. This will cause the service to process any files that are added to the queue for processing.

Note that this is just one possible implementation of a modular Node.js service for handling file processing tasks with caching and concurrency optimization. There are many other approaches and technologies that could be used, depending on your specific requirements and constraints."]

