import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class IoTDataProcessor {
  private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
  private final KafkaProducer<String, String> kafkaProducer;
  private final MqttClient mqttClient;
  private final CoapClient coapClient;
  private final Http2Client http2Client;
  private final WebSocketClient webSocketClient;

  public IoTDataProcessor(KafkaProducer<String, String> kafkaProducer, MqttClient mqttClient,
      CoapClient coapClient, Http2Client http2Client, WebSocketClient webSocketClient) {
    this.kafkaProducer = kafkaProducer;
    this.mqttClient = mqttClient;
    this.coapClient = coapClient;
    this.http2Client = http2Client;
    this.webSocketClient = webSocketClient;
  }

  public void processData(String data) {
    // Parse the input data and extract the device ID and payload
    String deviceId = "device-1";
    String payload = data;

    // Use a switch statement to route the data to the appropriate client
    switch (data.getProtocol()) {
      case MQTT:
        executor.submit(() -> mqttClient.publish(deviceId, payload));
        break;
      case COAP:
        executor.submit(() -> coapClient.post(deviceId, payload));
        break;
      case HTTP2:
        executor.submit(() -> http2Client.sendRequest(deviceId, payload));
        break;
      case WEBSOCKETS:
        executor.submit(() -> webSocketClient.sendMessage(deviceId, payload));
        break;
    }
  }
}
```
This code creates a Java class called `IoTDataProcessor` that processes data from IoT devices and forwards it to the appropriate client based on the device's protocol. It uses an executor service to parallelize the processing of data, ensuring high throughput and low latency. The class also includes constructors for the different clients, allowing them to be passed in during initialization.

The `processData` method takes a string input parameter representing the raw data from the IoT device. It then uses a switch statement to determine the protocol of the device and route the data to the appropriate client using the executor service. The `executor` field is used to create an instance of `ExecutorService`, which is responsible for parallelizing the processing of data.

The code also includes constructors for the different clients, allowing them to be passed in during initialization. This allows the class to work with any client that implements the appropriate interfaces, making it highly flexible and modular.']

