To ensure robustness against cascading failures, we implement retry logic with exponential backoff and circuit breaker patterns to manage transient network issues and overloading. We also monitor rate limit status across services and dynamically adjust streaming strategies to avoid exceeding service thresholds, considering the impact on overall system performance and user experience.

To optimize for performance under concurrent access and high load, we use data paging and streaming techniques to enhance performance and resource efficiency, and implement a memory pool allocator to reduce fragmentation and optimize allocation. We prioritize memory usage efficiency while maintaining speed, ensuring thread safety through lock-free data structures and fine-grained locking mechanisms as needed.

We design the library for seamless integration into larger systems, supporting updates and maintenance, and plan for scalability to accommodate growing datasets and increased request volumes by incorporating sharding and distributed processing strategies. We also consider the architectural implications of our design choices on the broader system, ensuring that the library acts as a plug-and-play component that can be easily replaced or extended in the future.

To evaluate trade-offs between efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability, we assess the impact of different codecs on processing time and memory consumption, selecting the most suitable codecs based on these factors. We also analyze how the library influences the broader system architecture and performance, ensuring it acts as a plug-and-play component that can be easily replaced or extended in the future.

To ensure reliability and performance under stress, we develop comprehensive unit and integration tests to cover edge cases like unsupported codecs, high concurrency, and high load conditions. We also use tools like profiling and benchmarking to optimize performance and identify bottlenecks.']

