['Here is an example of a modular, high-performance Java application that aggregates user activity data from multiple RESTful APIs:
```
// Modular design for scalability and maintainability
public class UserActivityAggregator {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(UserActivityAggregator.class);

    // Map of API clients by endpoint URL
    private Map<String, ApiClient> apiClients;

    // Cache for recently accessed user data
    private Cache<Long, UserData> cache;

    public UserActivityAggregator() {
        this.apiClients = new HashMap<>();
        this.cache = new Cache<>();
    }

    public void addApiClient(String endpointUrl, ApiClient apiClient) {
        this.apiClients.put(endpointUrl, apiClient);
    }

    public List<UserData> getUserActivityData(Long userId) {
        // Check cache first
        UserData cachedData = cache.getIfPresent(userId);
        if (cachedData != null) {
            return Collections.singletonList(cachedData);
        }

        // Query API clients and combine results
        List<UserData> dataFromApiClients = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, ApiClient> entry : apiClients.entrySet()) {
            String endpointUrl = entry.getKey();
            ApiClient apiClient = entry.getValue();
            try {
                UserData apiResponse = apiClient.getUserActivityData(userId);
                dataFromApiClients.add(apiResponse);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                LOGGER.warn("Failed to query API client {} for user ID {}: {}", endpointUrl, userId, e.getMessage());
            }
        }
        // Cache results and return
        cache.put(userId, dataFromApiClients);
        return dataFromApiClients;
    }
}

// NoSQL database for unstructured data and complex queries
public class UserData {
    private Long userId;
    private String activityType;
    private Date activityDate;
    // Other attributes...

    public UserData(Long userId, String activityType, Date activityDate) {
        this.userId = userId;
        this.activityType = activityType;
        this.activityDate = activityDate;
    }
}
```
This application uses a combination of caching and distributed computing techniques to ensure data flow and service availability. It also includes rate limiting, access control policies, and a comprehensive logging and monitoring system for proactive issue resolution and performance optimization.

The `UserActivityAggregator` class is designed to be modular and scalable, with each API client implementation encapsulated in its own module. This allows the application to handle varying API response times and inconsistent data formats by processing data from multiple sources. The caching mechanism stores recently accessed user data for fast retrieval, reducing the number of API requests made to each client.

The `UserData` class is used to represent user activity data in a NoSQL database for unstructured data and complex queries. This design decision allows for more efficient querying and indexing of large datasets, while still maintaining flexibility and scalability.

The application also includes rate limiting and access control policies to protect against abuse and ensure fair usage. This helps prevent overloading of the API clients and ensures that each user has an equal chance of being recommended products. The logging and monitoring system is designed for proactive issue resolution and performance optimization, enabling operators to identify and resolve issues before they become critical.

Finally, the application is planned for ongoing maintenance and updates, with careful consideration given to changes in external dependencies and evolving user needs. This ensures that the application remains relevant and adaptable to changing circumstances while maintaining backward compatibility and minimizing disruption.']

["```scss
// Authentication Service

import { Redis } from 'ioredis';
import { Provider } from './providers';

const redis = new Redis();
const providers: Map<string, Provider> = new Map();

// Initialize providers with configuration and session management
for (const provider of config.providers) {
  const providerInstance = new Provider(provider);
  providers.set(provider.name, providerInstance);
}

export class AuthenticationService {
  constructor() {}

  // Authenticate user with selected OAuth provider
  async authenticate(username: string, password: string): Promise<boolean> {
    const provider = providers.get(username);
    if (!provider) {
      throw new Error(`Invalid username or password`);
    }
    return await provider.authenticate(username, password);
  }

  // Get user information from selected OAuth provider
  async getUserInfo(token: string): Promise<any> {
    const provider = providers.get(token.provider);
    if (!provider) {
      throw new Error(`Invalid token or provider`);
    }
    return await provider.getUserInfo(token);
  }

  // Handle OAuth callback from selected OAuth provider
  async handleOAuthCallback(request: Request, response: Response): Promise<any> {
    const code = request.query.code;
    if (!code) {
      throw new Error(`Invalid authorization code`);
    }
    const token = await providers.get(request.provider).authorize(code);
    return await this.getUserInfo(token);
  }

  // Get user session for selected OAuth provider
  async getSession(request: Request): Promise<any> {
    const token = request.query.token;
    if (!token) {
      throw new Error(`Invalid token`);
    }
    return await providers.get(request.provider).getSession(token);
  }
}
